  "Question": "Solve the equation \\(4(x+2)^2=25\\). (Separate the two roots with a comma and write them as fractions in simplest form or integers.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1/2，-9/2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": true,
  "Solution": "First rewrite the equation as \\((x+2)^2=25/4\\). Taking square roots gives \\(x+2=\\pm5/2\\), so \\(x=1/2\\) or \\(x=-9/2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Factor the two squared quantities\n\nWrite \\(4(x+2)^2=[2(x+2)]^2\\), so moving \\(25=5^2\\) to the left reveals a difference of squares:\n\\[\n[2(x+2)]^2-5^2=(2x-1)(2x+9)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero if and only if at least one factor is zero. Therefore, \\(2x-1=0\\) or \\(2x+9=0\\), so the roots are \\(\\frac12,-\\frac92\\).",
